# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: epictetus-2.logic.r2 has "□inclines_to_i" and "□(in_flesh → ruling_in_flesh)" and "□(in_will → ruling_in_will)" and "□(in_externals → ruling_in_externals)"; epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-2.logic.r2.3 have "□inclines_to_i" and "in_flesh → ruling_in_flesh" and "in_will → ruling_in_will" and "in_externals → ruling_in_externals"; they differ when only "the I and the Mine are placed in the flesh", "the ruling power is in the flesh", "I am there where my will is", "the ruling power is in the will", "the I and the Mine are placed in externals", "the ruling power is in externals", "necessarily, the animal inclines to the place where the I and the Mine are placed", "necessarily, the animal inclines to the place where the I and the Mine are placed", "necessarily, (if the I and the Mine are placed in the flesh, then the ruling power is in the flesh)" and "necessarily, (if I am there where my will is, then the ruling power is in the will)" are true, which the second allows
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the I and the Mine are placed in the flesh", "the ruling power is in the flesh", "I am there where my will is", "the ruling power is in the will", "the I and the Mine are placed in externals", "the ruling power is in externals", "necessarily, the animal inclines to the place where the I and the Mine are placed", "necessarily, the animal inclines to the place where the I and the Mine are placed", "necessarily, the animal inclines to the place where the I and the Mine are placed", "necessarily, (if the I and the Mine are placed in the flesh, then the ruling power is in the flesh)" and "necessarily, (if I am there where my will is, then the ruling power is in the will)" are true and "necessarily, (if the I and the Mine are placed in externals, then the ruling power is in externals)" is false. Is S2, "For where the I and the Mine are placed, to that place of necessity the animal inclines; if in the flesh, there is the ruling power; if in the will, it is there; and if it is in externals, it is there.", true then?
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: epictetus-2.logic.r2 and epictetus-2.logic.r2.3 have "in_will → interest_is_virtue"; epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 has "in_will → interest_is_virtue" and "interest_is_virtue → good_relations" and "good_relations → in_will"; they differ when only "I am there where my will is" and "my interest will be to maintain the character of fidelity, modesty, patience, abstinence, active co-operation and observing my relations" are true, which the first allows
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps epictetus-2.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "I am there where my will is" and "my interest will be to maintain the character of fidelity, modesty, patience, abstinence, active co-operation and observing my relations" are true and "I shall be a friend, son and father such as I ought to be" is false. Is S3, "If then I am there where my will is, then only shall I be a friend such as I ought to be, and son, and father; for this will be my interest, to maintain the character of fidelity, of modesty, of patience, of abstinence, of active co-operation, of observing my relations (towards all).", true then?
epictetus-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: epictetus-2.logic.r2 has "interest_is_virtue → same_place" and "secured → good_relations"; epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "a man puts in the same place his interest, sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends", "sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends are all secured" and "my interest will be to maintain the character of fidelity, modesty, patience, abstinence, active co-operation and observing my relations" are true, which the second allows; epictetus-2.logic.r2.3 has "interest_is_virtue → good_relations"
epictetus-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: epictetus-2.logic.r2 takes for granted "if my interest will be to maintain the character of fidelity, modesty, patience, abstinence, active co-operation and observing my relations, then a man puts in the same place his interest, sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends"; epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-2.logic.r2.3 do not
epictetus-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-2.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if my interest will be to maintain the character of fidelity, modesty, patience, abstinence, active co-operation and observing my relations, then a man puts in the same place his interest, sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
epictetus-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: epictetus-2.logic.r2 takes for granted "if sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends are all secured, then I shall be a friend, son and father such as I ought to be"; epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-2.logic.r2.3 do not
epictetus-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-2.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if sanctity, goodness, country, parents and friends are all secured, then I shall be a friend, son and father such as I ought to be"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/negation: epictetus-2.logic.r2 P8 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P8 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if I put myself in one place and honesty in another, then the doctrine of Epicurus becomes strong"
epictetus-2.md:1: argue/negation: epictetus-2.logic.r2.2 P10 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P10 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if I put myself in one place and honesty in another, then the doctrine of Epicurus becomes strong"
